1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,973
The small mirror above your windscreen was

2
00:00:02,973 --> 00:00:05,593
first made famous by a racing driver,

3
00:00:05,593 --> 00:00:08,708
at the very first Indianapolis five hundred,

4
00:00:08,708 --> 00:00:10,054
in nineteen eleven.

5
00:00:10,234 --> 00:00:12,614
Your brakes were proven at Le Mans.

6
00:00:12,794 --> 00:00:15,777
The paddles behind the steering wheel came

7
00:00:15,777 --> 00:00:16,985
from Formula One.

8
00:00:17,165 --> 00:00:19,747
Your everyday car is full of parts

9
00:00:19,747 --> 00:00:21,191
that were invented,

10
00:00:21,191 --> 00:00:23,469
tested or perfected in racing.

11
00:00:23,649 --> 00:00:27,028
Today, ten race car parts hiding in

12
00:00:27,028 --> 00:00:28,380
your road car,

13
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:30,696
and why each one exists.

14
00:00:31,046 --> 00:00:33,206
Car makers have an old saying.

15
00:00:33,386 --> 00:00:34,714
Win on Sunday,

16
00:00:34,714 --> 00:00:36,138
sell on Monday.

17
00:00:36,318 --> 00:00:38,980
Racing is the harshest test a part

18
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:39,684
can face.

19
00:00:39,864 --> 00:00:42,581
Brakes are used to their limit every

20
00:00:42,581 --> 00:00:45,147
lap, engines run at full power for

21
00:00:45,147 --> 00:00:48,015
hours, and every gram and every second

22
00:00:48,015 --> 00:00:48,618
matters.

23
00:00:48,798 --> 00:00:51,029
If an idea survives racing,

24
00:00:51,029 --> 00:00:53,837
it can survive your daily commute.

25
00:00:54,017 --> 00:00:54,980
And if it wins,

26
00:00:54,980 --> 00:00:57,549
the marketing department wants it in the

27
00:00:57,549 --> 00:00:58,127
showroom.

28
00:00:58,477 --> 00:01:01,551
As always, every fact here comes from

29
00:01:01,551 --> 00:01:02,797
a named source,

30
00:01:02,797 --> 00:01:07,617
car makers, motorsport history and engineering references,

31
00:01:07,617 --> 00:01:10,110
all listed in the description.

32
00:01:10,290 --> 00:01:12,674
Let's start with the oldest one,

33
00:01:12,674 --> 00:01:13,494
the mirror.

34
00:01:13,843 --> 00:01:15,919
Why does your car have race car

35
00:01:15,919 --> 00:01:18,731
parts, starting with the rear view mirror?

36
00:01:18,911 --> 00:01:21,971
At the first Indianapolis five hundred,

37
00:01:21,971 --> 00:01:23,462
in nineteen eleven,

38
00:01:23,462 --> 00:01:26,757
drivers usually carried a riding mechanic,

39
00:01:26,757 --> 00:01:29,660
who looked back and warned them about

40
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:31,543
cars coming from behind.

41
00:01:31,723 --> 00:01:35,082
Ray Harroun drove his Marmon Wasp alone,

42
00:01:35,082 --> 00:01:36,425
a single seater.

43
00:01:36,605 --> 00:01:38,169
Instead of a mechanic,

44
00:01:38,169 --> 00:01:40,231
he fitted a mirror on a frame

45
00:01:40,231 --> 00:01:41,226
above the car.

46
00:01:41,576 --> 00:01:43,038
Harroun won the race,

47
00:01:43,038 --> 00:01:45,337
and he is often credited with the

48
00:01:45,337 --> 00:01:47,497
first use of a rear view mirror

49
00:01:47,497 --> 00:01:48,124
on a car.

50
00:01:48,304 --> 00:01:51,496
Other drivers complained that the single seat

51
00:01:51,496 --> 00:01:54,050
car was dangerous without a lookout.

52
00:01:54,230 --> 00:01:56,181
The mirror was his answer.

53
00:01:56,361 --> 00:01:58,895
The vibration on the brick track made

54
00:01:58,895 --> 00:01:59,991
the image shake,

55
00:01:59,991 --> 00:02:02,183
but it worked well enough to see

56
00:02:02,183 --> 00:02:03,211
who was coming.

57
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:05,125
Within a few years,

58
00:02:05,125 --> 00:02:08,583
mirrors were being sold as accessories for

59
00:02:08,583 --> 00:02:11,382
road cars, and later they became a

60
00:02:11,382 --> 00:02:14,346
legal requirement in many countries.

61
00:02:14,526 --> 00:02:18,063
Today, cars have three mirrors and often

62
00:02:18,063 --> 00:02:19,124
cameras too.

63
00:02:19,304 --> 00:02:21,990
But the basic idea is the same

64
00:02:21,990 --> 00:02:23,959
as in nineteen eleven.

65
00:02:24,139 --> 00:02:27,011
A driver who looks forward still needs

66
00:02:27,011 --> 00:02:28,748
to know what is behind.

67
00:02:29,099 --> 00:02:31,397
Why does your car have disc brakes?

68
00:02:31,577 --> 00:02:33,682
Old cars used drum brakes,

69
00:02:33,682 --> 00:02:36,597
with shoes pressing on the inside of

70
00:02:36,597 --> 00:02:37,649
a metal drum.

71
00:02:37,829 --> 00:02:40,170
They worked, but when they got hot

72
00:02:40,170 --> 00:02:41,685
from repeated braking,

73
00:02:41,685 --> 00:02:42,787
they lost power.

74
00:02:42,967 --> 00:02:45,556
Drivers called it brake fade.

75
00:02:45,736 --> 00:02:48,547
A disc brake squeezes a flat metal

76
00:02:48,547 --> 00:02:50,035
disc between pads,

77
00:02:50,035 --> 00:02:51,689
out in the open air,

78
00:02:51,689 --> 00:02:53,921
where it cools much better.

79
00:02:54,271 --> 00:02:57,138
Disc brakes were invented long before,

80
00:02:57,138 --> 00:02:59,251
but racing made them famous.

81
00:02:59,431 --> 00:03:02,309
At Le Mans in nineteen fifty-three,

82
00:03:02,309 --> 00:03:05,598
Jaguar raced its C-Type with disc brakes

83
00:03:05,598 --> 00:03:07,407
developed with Dunlop.

84
00:03:07,587 --> 00:03:10,982
The drivers could brake later and harder,

85
00:03:10,982 --> 00:03:12,142
lap after lap,

86
00:03:12,142 --> 00:03:13,218
without fade.

87
00:03:13,398 --> 00:03:15,117
Jaguar won the race,

88
00:03:15,117 --> 00:03:17,438
finishing first and second.

89
00:03:17,618 --> 00:03:20,347
Rivals could no longer ignore the discs.

90
00:03:20,697 --> 00:03:22,329
Within a few years,

91
00:03:22,329 --> 00:03:25,251
disc brakes appeared on road cars,

92
00:03:25,251 --> 00:03:28,173
first on fast sports cars and then

93
00:03:28,173 --> 00:03:29,462
on family cars.

94
00:03:29,642 --> 00:03:32,460
Today, almost every new car has discs

95
00:03:32,460 --> 00:03:34,669
at least on the front wheels,

96
00:03:34,669 --> 00:03:37,258
where most of the braking happens.

97
00:03:37,438 --> 00:03:39,734
When you stop at a red light,

98
00:03:39,734 --> 00:03:42,109
you are using an idea that won

99
00:03:42,109 --> 00:03:44,009
a twenty-four hour race.

100
00:03:44,359 --> 00:03:46,975
Why do modern car engines spray their

101
00:03:46,975 --> 00:03:49,096
fuel instead of sucking it in?

102
00:03:49,276 --> 00:03:52,016
For decades, cars used carburettors,

103
00:03:52,016 --> 00:03:54,603
which mixed fuel and air using the

104
00:03:54,603 --> 00:03:56,277
suction of the engine.

105
00:03:56,457 --> 00:03:57,689
They were simple,

106
00:03:57,689 --> 00:03:58,849
but not precise.

107
00:03:59,029 --> 00:04:02,452
Fuel injection sprays a measured amount of

108
00:04:02,452 --> 00:04:04,408
fuel, giving more power,

109
00:04:04,408 --> 00:04:07,260
cleaner burning and better economy.

110
00:04:07,611 --> 00:04:10,631
One of the first production cars with

111
00:04:10,631 --> 00:04:14,304
fuel injection was the Mercedes three hundred

112
00:04:14,304 --> 00:04:16,427
SL of nineteen fifty-four,

113
00:04:16,427 --> 00:04:18,059
the famous Gullwing.

114
00:04:18,239 --> 00:04:20,548
The road car was born from a

115
00:04:20,548 --> 00:04:23,599
racing car, the three hundred SL that

116
00:04:23,599 --> 00:04:26,898
won Le Mans and the Carrera Panamericana

117
00:04:26,898 --> 00:04:28,712
in nineteen fifty-two.

118
00:04:28,892 --> 00:04:31,115
Its mechanical injection,

119
00:04:31,115 --> 00:04:32,359
made by Bosch,

120
00:04:32,359 --> 00:04:36,005
sprayed fuel directly into the cylinders,

121
00:04:36,005 --> 00:04:39,828
a technology learned from aircraft engines.

122
00:04:40,179 --> 00:04:44,277
For years, injection stayed in expensive cars.

123
00:04:44,457 --> 00:04:46,546
Then emission rules arrived,

124
00:04:46,546 --> 00:04:51,322
and electronic injection replaced carburettors almost everywhere

125
00:04:51,322 --> 00:04:53,188
by the nineteen nineties.

126
00:04:53,368 --> 00:04:56,578
Direct injection, the same idea as the

127
00:04:56,578 --> 00:04:59,958
Gullwing, is now in millions of ordinary

128
00:04:59,958 --> 00:05:00,380
cars.

129
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:03,451
A system for winning races became a

130
00:05:03,451 --> 00:05:06,342
system for passing emissions tests.

131
00:05:06,692 --> 00:05:09,239
Why do many car engines have two

132
00:05:09,239 --> 00:05:12,105
camshafts on top and four valves per

133
00:05:12,105 --> 00:05:12,822
cylinder?

134
00:05:13,002 --> 00:05:16,060
Because more valves let the engine breathe

135
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:18,463
more air, and more air means more

136
00:05:18,463 --> 00:05:18,899
power.

137
00:05:19,079 --> 00:05:21,945
That layout was proven in racing more

138
00:05:21,945 --> 00:05:23,417
than a century ago,

139
00:05:23,417 --> 00:05:24,269
by Peugeot.

140
00:05:24,449 --> 00:05:27,518
Its nineteen twelve Grand Prix car had

141
00:05:27,518 --> 00:05:30,909
two overhead camshafts and four valves per

142
00:05:30,909 --> 00:05:31,636
cylinder.

143
00:05:31,986 --> 00:05:34,918
The Peugeot won the French Grand Prix

144
00:05:34,918 --> 00:05:36,424
in nineteen twelve,

145
00:05:36,424 --> 00:05:39,991
and the Indianapolis five hundred in nineteen

146
00:05:39,991 --> 00:05:40,705
thirteen.

147
00:05:40,885 --> 00:05:43,543
Engineers around the world studied it.

148
00:05:43,723 --> 00:05:46,039
The design let the engine rev higher

149
00:05:46,039 --> 00:05:48,613
and breathe better than anything else at

150
00:05:48,613 --> 00:05:49,192
the time.

151
00:05:49,372 --> 00:05:53,155
Racing engines have used twin camshafts ever

152
00:05:53,155 --> 00:05:56,767
since, from Grand Prix cars to motorbikes.

153
00:05:57,118 --> 00:05:58,329
In road cars,

154
00:05:58,329 --> 00:05:59,726
it took longer,

155
00:05:59,726 --> 00:06:03,638
because twin cam engines were more complex

156
00:06:03,638 --> 00:06:04,943
and expensive.

157
00:06:05,123 --> 00:06:07,661
But from the nineteen eighties,

158
00:06:07,661 --> 00:06:11,183
four valve, twin camshaft engines spread to

159
00:06:11,183 --> 00:06:12,739
normal family cars.

160
00:06:12,919 --> 00:06:16,226
If your car's engine says sixteen valves

161
00:06:16,226 --> 00:06:17,301
on the cover,

162
00:06:17,301 --> 00:06:20,195
that is a four cylinder engine with

163
00:06:20,195 --> 00:06:21,601
four valves each,

164
00:06:21,601 --> 00:06:24,495
a direct descendant of a racing car

165
00:06:24,495 --> 00:06:27,141
from before the First World War.

166
00:06:27,491 --> 00:06:30,357
Why do some modern gearboxes have two

167
00:06:30,357 --> 00:06:31,055
clutches?

168
00:06:31,235 --> 00:06:33,487
It is a dual clutch gearbox.

169
00:06:33,667 --> 00:06:36,486
One clutch handles the odd gears,

170
00:06:36,486 --> 00:06:38,195
one, three and five.

171
00:06:38,375 --> 00:06:41,072
The other handles the even gears,

172
00:06:41,072 --> 00:06:42,543
two, four and six.

173
00:06:42,723 --> 00:06:45,035
While you drive in one gear,

174
00:06:45,035 --> 00:06:48,007
the next one is already selected and

175
00:06:48,007 --> 00:06:48,667
waiting.

176
00:06:48,847 --> 00:06:51,819
The change is just one clutch opening

177
00:06:51,819 --> 00:06:53,667
while the other closes,

178
00:06:53,667 --> 00:06:55,755
in a fraction of a second.

179
00:06:56,106 --> 00:06:58,799
The idea was developed for racing.

180
00:06:58,979 --> 00:07:01,041
In the nineteen eighties,

181
00:07:01,041 --> 00:07:04,339
Porsche used its PDK dual clutch gearbox

182
00:07:04,339 --> 00:07:06,731
on the nine five six and nine

183
00:07:06,731 --> 00:07:08,215
six two race cars,

184
00:07:08,215 --> 00:07:11,102
and Audi tried a dual clutch system

185
00:07:11,102 --> 00:07:12,586
on its rally cars.

186
00:07:12,766 --> 00:07:15,013
The goal was to keep the turbo

187
00:07:15,013 --> 00:07:17,858
spinning and never lose power during a

188
00:07:17,858 --> 00:07:18,757
gear change.

189
00:07:18,937 --> 00:07:22,355
The early systems were heavy and complex,

190
00:07:22,355 --> 00:07:24,440
but they proved the idea.

191
00:07:24,790 --> 00:07:28,149
When electronics became fast and cheap enough,

192
00:07:28,149 --> 00:07:30,560
the dual clutch came to the road.

193
00:07:30,740 --> 00:07:34,345
Volkswagen launched its DSG gearbox in two

194
00:07:34,345 --> 00:07:38,121
thousand three, and Porsche brought PDK back

195
00:07:38,121 --> 00:07:40,782
in road cars a few years later.

196
00:07:40,962 --> 00:07:44,369
Today you find dual clutch gearboxes in

197
00:07:44,369 --> 00:07:48,211
hot hatches, supercars and even small family

198
00:07:48,211 --> 00:07:48,648
cars.

199
00:07:48,828 --> 00:07:49,958
The gearbox races,

200
00:07:49,958 --> 00:07:51,904
even when you are just going to

201
00:07:51,904 --> 00:07:52,218
work.

202
00:07:52,569 --> 00:07:55,253
Why do many road cars have paddles

203
00:07:55,253 --> 00:07:57,305
behind the steering wheel?

204
00:07:57,485 --> 00:07:59,494
They came from Formula One.

205
00:07:59,674 --> 00:08:01,436
In nineteen eighty-nine,

206
00:08:01,436 --> 00:08:03,491
Ferrari raced the six forty,

207
00:08:03,491 --> 00:08:05,326
designed by John Barnard,

208
00:08:05,326 --> 00:08:08,482
with a semi automatic gearbox controlled by

209
00:08:08,482 --> 00:08:10,611
two paddles behind the wheel.

210
00:08:10,791 --> 00:08:13,416
The driver no longer needed a clutch

211
00:08:13,416 --> 00:08:15,020
pedal or a gear lever,

212
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:17,354
and could keep both hands on the

213
00:08:17,354 --> 00:08:17,791
wheel.

214
00:08:18,142 --> 00:08:21,111
Many people expected the system to break,

215
00:08:21,111 --> 00:08:22,995
and at first it often did.

216
00:08:23,175 --> 00:08:25,937
But Nigel Mansell won the very first

217
00:08:25,937 --> 00:08:26,935
race with it,

218
00:08:26,935 --> 00:08:27,702
in Brazil.

219
00:08:27,882 --> 00:08:29,587
Within a few years,

220
00:08:29,587 --> 00:08:32,817
every Formula One team used paddles.

221
00:08:32,997 --> 00:08:35,712
Ferrari then brought the idea to the

222
00:08:35,712 --> 00:08:37,975
road in nineteen ninety-seven,

223
00:08:37,975 --> 00:08:39,936
in the F three five five F

224
00:08:39,936 --> 00:08:43,104
one, the name telling buyers exactly where

225
00:08:43,104 --> 00:08:44,084
it came from.

226
00:08:44,435 --> 00:08:47,923
Today, paddles are in cars of every

227
00:08:47,923 --> 00:08:48,521
price.

228
00:08:48,701 --> 00:08:49,884
In most of them,

229
00:08:49,884 --> 00:08:52,396
they control an automatic gearbox,

230
00:08:52,396 --> 00:08:54,982
letting the driver choose the gear.

231
00:08:55,162 --> 00:08:57,311
They are not always faster than letting

232
00:08:57,311 --> 00:08:58,413
the computer decide.

233
00:08:58,593 --> 00:09:01,571
But the feeling of clicking a paddle,

234
00:09:01,571 --> 00:09:03,664
like a Formula One driver,

235
00:09:03,664 --> 00:09:04,549
sells cars.

236
00:09:04,729 --> 00:09:06,013
Win on Sunday,

237
00:09:06,013 --> 00:09:07,388
sell on Monday.

238
00:09:07,738 --> 00:09:10,384
Why are many supercars built around a

239
00:09:10,384 --> 00:09:11,743
carbon fibre shell?

240
00:09:11,923 --> 00:09:15,279
Because carbon fibre is extremely stiff and

241
00:09:15,279 --> 00:09:16,997
strong for its weight.

242
00:09:17,177 --> 00:09:19,215
In nineteen eighty-one,

243
00:09:19,215 --> 00:09:22,405
McLaren raced the MP four slash one,

244
00:09:22,405 --> 00:09:25,241
the first Formula One car with a

245
00:09:25,241 --> 00:09:29,495
chassis made entirely of carbon fibre composite.

246
00:09:29,675 --> 00:09:32,421
Rivals worried it would shatter in a

247
00:09:32,421 --> 00:09:32,879
crash.

248
00:09:33,059 --> 00:09:36,507
Instead, it proved incredibly strong.

249
00:09:36,858 --> 00:09:38,312
At Monza that year,

250
00:09:38,312 --> 00:09:41,297
John Watson crashed heavily and the car

251
00:09:41,297 --> 00:09:44,205
broke apart, but the carbon tub around

252
00:09:44,205 --> 00:09:46,424
him held, and he walked away.

253
00:09:46,604 --> 00:09:48,178
Within a few years,

254
00:09:48,178 --> 00:09:50,994
every Formula One car had a carbon

255
00:09:50,994 --> 00:09:52,154
fibre chassis.

256
00:09:52,334 --> 00:09:54,938
It became one of the biggest safety

257
00:09:54,938 --> 00:09:57,988
improvements in the history of the sport,

258
00:09:57,988 --> 00:10:01,187
because the survival cell around the driver

259
00:10:01,187 --> 00:10:03,270
no longer folded in a crash.

260
00:10:03,621 --> 00:10:05,492
In nineteen ninety-two,

261
00:10:05,492 --> 00:10:08,177
McLaren built the F one road car,

262
00:10:08,177 --> 00:10:10,700
one of the first road cars with

263
00:10:10,700 --> 00:10:12,978
a full carbon fibre chassis.

264
00:10:13,158 --> 00:10:15,989
Today, supercars from McLaren,

265
00:10:15,989 --> 00:10:19,292
Ferrari and others use carbon tubs,

266
00:10:19,292 --> 00:10:22,973
and carbon panels appear on many sports

267
00:10:22,973 --> 00:10:23,445
cars.

268
00:10:23,625 --> 00:10:25,680
It is still expensive,

269
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:28,763
because each part is laid by hand

270
00:10:28,763 --> 00:10:29,697
and baked.

271
00:10:29,877 --> 00:10:32,917
But the material that saved Formula One

272
00:10:32,917 --> 00:10:35,879
drivers now holds supercar owners too.

273
00:10:36,229 --> 00:10:38,477
Why do some road cars have a

274
00:10:38,477 --> 00:10:40,083
spoiler on the back?

275
00:10:40,263 --> 00:10:41,336
On a fast car,

276
00:10:41,336 --> 00:10:44,400
air flowing over the curved roof creates

277
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:45,702
lift at the rear,

278
00:10:45,702 --> 00:10:48,537
making the back end light and nervous

279
00:10:48,537 --> 00:10:49,609
at high speed.

280
00:10:49,789 --> 00:10:52,880
A spoiler disturbs that flow and cuts

281
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:53,632
the lift.

282
00:10:53,812 --> 00:10:55,917
In nineteen seventy-three,

283
00:10:55,917 --> 00:10:59,075
Porsche gave the nine eleven Carrera RS

284
00:10:59,075 --> 00:11:02,233
two point seven a famous spoiler called

